  },
  {
    "check_id": "W7B8:W7B8:CHK-0126",
    "title": "Supporting Check: Is the commitment_indices mapping bijective on equivalence classes?",
    "notes": "Source: PROP-W5B14-13, Trust Boundary: tb-002. Bijective property ensures no loss of information and correct weight distribution in batch verification.",
    "test_procedure": "1. Review the deduplication algorithm to understand how equivalence classes are formed. 2. Verify that each equivalence class (set of identical commitments) maps to exactly one deduplicated commitment. 3. Verify the reverse: each deduplicated commitment corresponds to exactly one equivalence class. 4. Test with commitments that are byte-wise different but semantically equivalent (if applicable). 5. Verify no aliasing occurs where different original indices incorrectly share the same deduplicated index when they shouldn't. 6. Check that the mapping is consistent across multiple calls with the same input."
  },
  {
    "check_id": "W7B8:W7B8:CHK-0127",
    "title": "Supporting Check: Can a malicious P2P peer exploit commitment deduplication to forge valid proofs?",
    "notes": "Source: PROP-W5B14-13, Trust Boundary: tb-002. Attacker-controlled input via P2P could exploit off-by-one to accept invalid proofs.",
    "test_procedure": "1. Identify how P2P peers submit batches containing cell KZG proofs. 2. Analyze whether a peer can control the ordering of commitments in a batch. 3. Attempt to construct a batch where: (a) duplicate commitments are positioned to cause index confusion, (b) invalid proofs are included that would pass if indices are misaligned. 4. Verify that P2P gossip validation catches malformed batches before deduplication. 5. Test if byte length checks are sufficient to prevent manipulation. 6. Verify that even with attacker-controlled commitment ordering, the verification equation remains sound."
  },
  {
    "check_id": "W7B8:W7B8:CHK-0128",
    "title": "Supporting Check: What is the impact on validator-node if commitment deduplication fails?",
    "notes": "Source: PROP-W5B14-13, Nodes: actor-validator-node. Impact assessment for validator-level consequences of deduplication bugs.",
    "test_procedure": "1. Trace the flow from verify_cell_kzg_proof_batch to validator node decision-making. 2. Identify what actions validators take based on batch verification results. 3. Determine if accepting an invalid proof could lead to: (a) accepting invalid data availability proofs, (b) incorrect attestations, (c) consensus violations. 4. Assess the blast radius: would a single corrupted batch affect only one validator or propagate across the network? 5. Check if there are secondary validation steps that would catch a bypassed proof."
  }
]

